        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 12,
        "titulo": "Four shortcuts for reading and listening",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: cognates: many French words look like English ones (-tion = -tion: «la décision», «l'information»; -ité = -ity: «la qualité», «la liberté»; -eur = -or: «le professeur», «l'acteur»). STEP 2: word families: «travailler, travail, travailleur, travaillé» share the same root. If you know one, you understand the others. STEP 3: prefixes: «re-» = again («refaire»), «in-/im-» = negation («impossible»), «dé-» = undoing («défaire»), «pré-» = before («prévoir»). STEP 4: context: read the whole sentence and ask yourself what kind of word is missing (person, action, place). STEP 5: when listening, look first for the words you know, the numerals and the proper names; the essentials are usually there. STEP 6: do not stop at an unknown word: keep going and come back to it later.",
        "comparacao": "in English, the endings «-tion» and «-ity» have French counterparts («-tion», «-té»). That gives you a huge passive vocabulary for free. Only beware of the false friends, which we cover next.",
